  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis at midday \nshow ridging is spread out from the southern plains eastward through \nthe east coast, with a strong cut-off low over the Pacific \nNorthwest. At the surface, broad low pressure extends across nearly \nthe entirety of the High Plains, supporting 10-20 mph southerly \nwinds in southwest KS. Short range guidance indicates no change in \nthe overall pattern will occur through the remainder of the day, and \nanother day of strong solar insolation with limited cloud cover will \nresult in afternoon temperatures peaking in the 100-105 degree \nrange once again. Later this afternoon, HREF members suggest \nscattered showers and weak thunderstorms will meander into \nwestern KS after 22Z. Given meager upper level support and weak \ndeep-layer shear/instability, this activity will remain \ncomfortably sub-severe. Overnight, partly to mostly cloudy \nskies and light south-southeast winds are expected with lows \ndropping into the 65-75 degree range.\n\nRinse and repeat the forecast for Saturday as strong upper level \nridging promotes another warm, sunny day with highs in the 97-102 \ndegree range, along with similar weak convective activity favoring \nour western zones during the afternoon. Sunday through the end of \nthe period, medium range ensembles agree the upper ridge will be \nvirtually stationary, wobbling only slightly from east to west. This \nsynoptic evolution will lock in above normal temperatures along with \nlimited precipitation chances across the central plains for the \nforeseeable future. Beyond the scope of this forecast period, long \nrange ensembles are hinting at a possible break in the heat sometime \naround late next week/early the following week. Now, any forecast at \nthis range is highly dubious, and should be taken with several \ngrains of salt, but there is at least a modicum of hope that fall \ntemperatures are on the distant horizon.",
